Date: 19 Jan 94 13:49:43 EST
From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Subject: Claim: Extropians *Is* Becoming a Religion

Tim May writes: <I've seen nothing in the "Extropian Principles" that is
especially profound... To suggest that these "discoveries" are to be
communicated to us sounds like High Priests and Talmudic scholars passing on
sacred revelations.  Max publishes a fine little magazine, but to suggest that
he makes discoveries and then communicates them to us through newer versions of
the Extropian Principles is comical.  The Principles are already creating a
dogma. People attempt to win their arguments by quoting from the Sacred
Principles.  Adding epistemology [to FAQ]... runs the risk of becoming the
Extropian Catechism.  The schismatics could nail their "Ninety-Five Theses" to
the door of the church the Assembler Multitudes meets in, declaring that Max is
no longer needed as an intermediary to communicate the Truths of Extropianism to
the masses, that every Upload is Capable of Interpreting the Extropian Word
itself.  I'll take a megabyte a week of sometimes interesting conversation on
this list over some platitudes about Boundless Expansion written on some index
cards.>

I am shocked to see Tim write this material.  I was under the impression (as
related to me by Geoff Dale and Romana Machado) that you had to get permission
from the Board of Directors of ExI before you could post any comment whatsoever
on Extropian philosophy.  I guess I have been misled.  As Geoff wrote: "I would
hold that the primary value for choosing what goes under the label extropianism
should rationally be are the opinions of the people that run the Extropy
Institute and founded the philosophy."  As Romana wrote: "No doubt Reilly, and
others to follow, will continue to promote various concepts as 'extropian'.
There is no human pursuit that does not have imitators, the perpetually
confused, crazies, and charlatans."

I agree with Tim about exercising care in picking an epistemology for Extropian
philosophy (likewise a cosmology, a political system, an economic system, moral
values, self-definition, etc.).  Philosophies should serve a purpose.  Far too
often, philosophies are patchwork contraptions of odds and ends that don't
always serve the purpose and often work at cross-purposes from within the
philosophy.  I have seen too much of the "French disease" in List postings and
material in Extropy Journal (this disease is also known as 'compost-modernism').
A purge is in order for purification.  Perhaps Tim could crank up the
"Ninety-Five Theses" for posting <g>.  Tim, I actually found a twinge of a smile
hovering around the corner of my mouth while reading your post, thank you from a
fellow KMA Club member.

