Tim May, I'm glad you took the time to respond to my post. I don't
mind annoying folks, I just don't like them to get annoyed for the
wrong reasons.

> Put this way, my issue is that some of the "honest and
> sincere" emotional admissions on this list, particularly the ones
> talked about so far, are simply bad manners. 

I translate "are bad manners" to "Tim May doesn't like them", or at
best, "Tim May doesn't like them, and he thinks most people on the
list don't either." I have no problems with Kennita's open admissions.
I doubt that they are much of an issue for many on the list. Some
people might even like them. I've found them interesting at times.

Suppose we are at a party. A small circle develops around Kennita.
She begins to discuss personal issues. Joe finds it uninteresting,
perhaps even distasteful, and so wanders off to mingle elsewhere. Jack
feels likewise, but instead tells Kennita she has bad manners. Joe has
exhibited good breeding (I like his solution) while Jack has bad
manners (I don't like what he did).

If everyone truly feels and acts as Jack does, Kennita will get the
message soon enough when she becomes a circle of one.  If some people
feel as Jim does, who enjoys Kennita's discussion, has Jack
contributed much to the party by shutting down Kennita? Is it Jacks
intent to shut down discussions which he doesn't like? Can't Jack just
yak and let yak, especially when he has the option of not listening to
yak he doesn't like? Jack?
 
> The second paragraph, about control of the feebleminded, still 
> mystifies me. I will take from Dan's e-mail to me that he was not 
> calling me feebleminded.

Correct, I was not.

> But was he saying that I am using the term
> "appropriate" as a means of controlling the feebleminded?

Yes. Apparently you don't see it that way. I see the use of the term
"inappropriate" usually just as a statement of personal preference,
and at best as an inference about the personal preferences of the
members of a certain group. The feebleminded, as I call them, would
see inappropriate, or let us say bad manners, as some kind of
objectified illegitimacy to their actions, a cosmic "Thou shalt not!"
very similar to a moral proscription.

Of course, we would all like all conversations at the parties we
attend to be of interest to ourselves. We might even try to control
those talking about things we aren't interested in by telling them
they have bad manners. If our victim is particularly feebleminded, they
may fly into self recrimination over their incorrect behaviour. If
they are not, we have essentially just told someone "Shut up, please,
I don't like what you have to say." (Which is how I would take it if I
thought the speaker understood the issue.)

I originally said:
> > Just goes to show that most discourse is about control of the
> > feebleminded who accept imperatives if the right ritual incantations
> > are used - words like good, bad, immoral, appropriate are particularly
> > powerful magic with such people.

To demystify Tim over my meaning in the last paragraph, I'm just
saying that a lot of discourse comes in the form of disguised
imperatives which gain much of their power over the listeners by using
special words which have motive force for some people. Perhaps we
disagree whether your use of inappropriate falls into this category.

