Date: Wed, 19 Jan 94 13:54:59 PST
From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Subject: If Uploads Come First

Nancy Lebovitz writes:
>If I understand Robin's argument correctly, he proposes that reproduction
>by making uploads will be so much cheaper and more profitable than
>biological reproduction that people (whether flesh or silicon) will
>live at the Malthusian limit. I wonder if this is likely--the rewards
>for finding methods to use resources more efficiently will be so great
>that the majority may never be in the situation of working constantly
>just to stay alive.

If there are high "rewards for finding methods to use resources more
efficiently" then lots of capital shoulld be invested into such
search, and the total growth rate may be large.  But the growth rate
can be high and the per capita wealth be either low or high - there
is no necessary connection between the two.  The main connection is
between the wealth growth rate and the population growth rate.  If the
wealth growth rate is larger, per capita wealth rises.  If not, it falls.

Robin Hanson

