In the main I agree with this post, but from my perspective as the regular
host of diverse and sometimes large parties, I've noticed that if there's
less than about 20 people present, one whining blabbermouth can cast a pall
on the entire affair. This individual is often illiterate at reading social
cues, knowing how to contain themselves and being a participant instead of
a tragic character hungry for the spotlight. 

Sometimes it's possible to use humor to stop the gushing (presuming most
other participants are made uncomfortable by it), sometimes its necessary
to take the person aside and try to distract them as one would with a
child, other times to ask them to stop. Once in a very great while it's
necessary to be direct and ask the person to put a cork in it. Rare, but
sometimes it has to happen on behalf of the other guests, especially if you
want the other guests to have a good time and come back again. Solution:
don't invite whiners to small parties or intimate gatherings a second time.
And as a host, pay careful attention to which people congregate with which
people.

