Try _The Man-Eating Leopard of Rudraprayag_ (I believe that's the one
you're talking about) by Jim Corbett, a hunter-turned-photographer and
an excellent jungle tracker.  Grew up in Imperial India and worked on
the railways.  Almost incidently he hunted man-eating tigers (and the
occasional leopard) and later wrote a number of books about his
experiences.  The head count was over 150 to the leopard, BTW.

Also by Jim Corbett are _Man-Eaters of Kumaon_, _The Temple Tiger and
_More Man-Eaters of Kumaon_.  Imaginative titles, eh?

>> In support of [the taste-bad theory of human immunity to predators],
>> big cats will rarely attack chimpanzees [...] and will often discard
>> the kill after inspection.

For what it's worth popular mythology says that an animal (including
human) that tastes "long-pork" is reluctant to give it up.  One of the
man-eaters that Corbett hunted became a man-eater through accidentally
killing a meddling human.  The human corpse was eaten as an afterthought
and she decided she liked the taste....  [Okay, an inference, I know,
but a reasonable one, I feel]  The cubs of man-eaters often become
man-eaters in turn and particularly dangerous.  An acquired taste,
perhaps?

And who says chimps taste like humans, anyway?

