Date: Wed, 19 Jan 94 14:48:31 PST
From: edgar@spectrx.saigon.com (Edgar W. Swank)
Subject: Children as Property

Dani Elder makes some good points msg #94-1-81 of Jan 14:

    > The are two phases to consider.  In "infancy" the child is incapable
    > of communication beyond crying and smiling and is certainly incapable
    > of contract, so parents assume certain "rights" to make decisions for
    > the child.  It seems to me that whatever parental "rights" are, the
    > parents do own those and can free sell them or give them away. So
    > parents should be free to sell their children in the sense of selling
    > their parental rights to some other party (but not to sell the child
    > into slavery for example).

    If an entity is incapable of making contracts, then we categorize
    it as 'chattel', i.e.  property.  For example, dogs, cats, and
    cows fall into this category.  We don't seem to have a problem
    buying, selling, and doing various other things with these
    animals.

Actually, in the real world, we sometimes do. A mistreated dog or cat
may attack you or just run off.  While I would agree with you that
third parties have no legitimate interest in interfering in the
relationship of an owner and his "chattel", this often occurs, vis.
"cruelty to animals" laws, etc.

    If you tell me that a child has the 'potential' of becoming
    intelligent enough to contract, well then so does a dog, given
    sufficient input from advanced technology.  The child can't do it
    itself, it needs lots of resources provided by adults and exposure
    to the right environment at the right times (like language
    exposure at the right age).  Even my Macintosh, which is only a
    baby (about 1 year old), might grow up to be an intelligent being
    (give it about 20 more years of upgrades - that should be about a
    factor of 1000 improvement).

Good points, except that a human infant will become competent at some
point with 99.9%+ probability. No-one has ever observed a
(non-fictional) dog, or even a Mac, do so.

    > At some age, which probably varies with the individual child (you
    > parents help me here), but long before the legal age of adulthood,
    > children become capable of communicating reasonably complex ideas and
    > linking speech to action.  They become capable of understand the
    > concept of a promise of future action for some present reward or vice
    > versa. At this time, parental "rights" to make decisions for children
    > (should) disappear because they are capable of making their own
    > decisions (at least until proven otherwise).
    >
    Then, you seem to be saying that it is competent intelligence that
    makes one a self-owner.  I will grant you this point.  But until a
    child can claim this right, I would place the child legally in the
    same class as the other semi-intelligent animals.  The parents
    have a limited-term property right in the child that ends with
    childhood.  After that point, they are dealing with an independant
    legal entity and can contract as they wish with each other.

I am persuaded to agree with this, except I would replace "childhood"
with "infancy."

    My experience is the age would be 12-15 depending on the child.

With respect to your experience, this is way off.  By age 6, children
are attending school, which requires fairly advanced communication
skills.  All that's required, in my view, to demonstrate a capacity
for self-ownership is to communicate a demand for it.

How old does a kid have to be to approach a policeman and say, "I
don't want to live with Mommy & Daddy any more."?  Mommy & Daddy's
"chattel" rights end right there.

