From: "Kathleen Cadmus" <kcadmus@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: SOC: Extropian relationships

My thanks to Jamie for that eloquent and moving paean to passionate love! I
share his skepticism about whether relationship quality can be reduced to a
formal system. What we seem to want in relationships may simply be too
subjective and individualized. But I do know that Jamie beautifully described
what I want, by whatever name we want to call it. Just reading it warmed my
heart. (Very welcome on an evening that saw the temperature here in Columbus
drop to twenty-two below! Is that thread about "Where to Live" still
active?) My spouse, while far from a natural extropian, gives me this and
much more. I'm well satisfied!

In response to some very interesting observations by Nick, Geoff astutely
observes that the past (whether inherited memes or personal experiences) can
be a grand teacher of what good (extropian?) relationships are *not*. And
perhaps that is the best we can do. The formalist approach to defining
extropian relationships reminds me of Columbus' discovery of the Orient. I
suggest that we first explore with open minds and open hearts and much later
we can label what we've found (if it is useful to do so).

I might offer you testimony about how my first marriage, in all its
libertarian splendor, turned into a pile of crap. But I'm not sure that
would be useful or even well-mannered. My understanding of what a great
relationship can be is 99% based on my good fortune in unexpectedly landing
in one. So now maybe I'm contradicting what I said above, but I suggest that
the most successful strategy is to rapidly "evolve" your understanding of
what "works" for you in relationships by charging ahead at full steam.

On that note, best wishes to Vince and Romana in your quest!
